{"id": "991", "revid": "2236161", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=991", "title": "Amesbury (Massachusetts)", "text": "Amesbury is a town in Massachusetts with a population of 17,000 (2020).\nMerrimac is a town with a population of 7,000 (2020).\nUnderstand.\nFormerly a farming and mill town, modern Amesbury is for the most part residential. It is one of two northernmost towns in Massachusetts, the other being neighboring Salisbury.\nGet in.\nThere are daily buses from Haverhill and from Salisbury via Newburyport. Buses stops at, which is just a few hundred meters from Main street."}
{"id": "994", "revid": "2242523", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=994", "title": "Amharic phrasebook", "text": "\"\"Ethiopian Language\" redirects here. For other languages of this multilingual country see Ethiopia#Talk\"\nAmharic (\u12a0\u121b\u122d\u129b) is the \"lingua franca\" and first official language of Ethiopia. It is spoken natively by the Amhara people in northern and central Ethiopia, and as a second language by most other Ethiopians.\nPronunciation guide.\nConsonants and vowels.\nConsonants.\n\u1205 h as in hat\n\u120d l as in lamp\n\u1215 h as in hat\n\u121d m as in mat\n\u1225 s as in six\n\u1228 r as in rabbit\n\u1235 s as in six\n\u123d sh as in ash\n\u1245 k pronounced at the back of the throat\n\u1265 b as in bat\n\u1275 t as in track\n\u127d ch as in chat\n\u1285 h as in hat\n\u1295 n as in night\n\u129d ny as in canyon\n\u12ad k as in dock\n\u12bd h as in hat\n\u12cd w as in whale\n\u12d5 i pronounced at the back of throat\n\u12dd z as in zebra\n\u12e5 zh as in pleasure\n\u12ed y as in yoke\n\u12f5 d as in dog\n\u1305 j as in jaguar\n\u130d g as in goat\n\u1325 t pronounced by stressing\n\u132d ch pronounced by stressing\n\u1335 p pronounced by stressing\n\u133d sound between t and s\n\u1345 sound between t and s\n\u1348 f as in fat\n\u1355 p as in pencil\n\u126d v as in vase \nVowels.\n\u12a7 e as in commander\n\u12a1 u as in Uzbek \n\u12a2 i as in inject\n\u12a0 a as in father\n\u12a4 e as in beg\n\u12a5 i as in see, but further back in the mouth\n\u12a6 o as in orange\nAs in Japanese, Arabic or Hebrew, Amharic vowels are not written separately; vowels are marks attached to consonants. See this.\nPhrase list.\nBasics.\n\"Some in this still need to be translated. If you know anything about this language, you can by and.\""}
